*======================================================================
* pool.a99 - a page pool: hand out and take back SAMS pages
*
* The kernel keeps a bitmap of up to POOLMX pages (a bit per page, 1 = free:
* 512 bytes for the 4096 of a 16M card, at POOLBM, in the kernel's own pages;
* an application that never pools more pages than it says in app.json's
* "pool_max_pages" has a smaller bitmap and the kernel more room).
* PINIT says which pages the pool manages (normally everything from
* PGFREE, the first page the build leaves free, up to NPAGES, or less if
* the application keeps some pages for itself).  PALLOC gives out the
* lowest free page, PRELS takes one back.  A page's contents are not
* touched: the application clears what it needs.  Pages the pool hands
* out are not mapped anywhere; map them with MAPE, MAPF or MAPPG.
*======================================================================
* A POOL OF PAGES, for programs that need memory without choosing where.  A
* SAMS card of 1M has 256 pages, one of 16M has 4,096; a program that wants a
* buffer larger than slot A can hold asks the pool for a page and gets a
* number; it maps the number into a window to use it (MAPE, MAPF) and gives
* the number back when it has finished (PRELS).  The pool is a bitmap: one bit
* per page, 1 meaning free, so 4,096 pages need 512 bytes (POOLBM).  The
* bitmap is kept in the kernel's own pages, out of your 7K.
*
* The pool does not clear a page, map a page or know what a page holds: it
* only says which numbers are in use.  Two programs that share the card (the
* workbench's editor and a program under test) must agree on who owns which
* pages; the workbench's way is described in examples/workbench/ed.a99
* (POOLUP) and docs/WORKBENCH.md.

* PINIT - the pool manages pages R0 up to (not including) R1; all free.
* Pages outside the range are never handed out, and none from POOLMX up (the
* bitmap has no bit for them: app.json's "pool_max_pages", 4096 by default).
* Uses R0-R6.  Leaf.
PINIT  MOV  R11,R6
       CI   R1,POOLMX          no page the bitmap has no bit for
       JLE  PI0
       LI   R1,POOLMX
PI0    MOV  R0,@POOLLO
       MOV  R1,@POOLHI
       LI   R2,POOLBM          clear the bitmap
       LI   R3,POOLWD
PI1    CLR  *R2+
       DEC  R3
       JNE  PI1
       CLR  @FREECT
PI2    C    R0,R1              each page of the range: free
       JHE  PI9
       BL   @PSET
       INC  @FREECT
       INC  R0
       JMP  PI2
PI9    B    *R6

* The routines here avoid the software stack and keep their return address in
* a spare register instead (MOV R11,R3 ... B *R3), because they are leaf
* routines that call each other.  BL changes R11, so a routine that calls
* another with BL must save R11 first; saving it in a register is cheaper than
* a push and is safe as long as the callee does not use that register (the
* 'Uses' lists say which).
* PSET - mark page R0 free in the bitmap.  Leaf (R11 is kept in R3).
* Uses R2, R3, R4, R5.
PSET   MOV  R11,R3
       BL   @PBIT              R2 = the bitmap byte's address, R4 = its mask
       SOCB R4,*R2
       B    *R3

* Page n is bit (n mod 8) of byte (n / 8), counted from the left (the top bit
* of the byte is page 0): the code computes the byte's address (SRL R2,3:
* divide by 8) and builds the mask by shifting a 1 in the top bit right n mod
* 8 places.  COC and SZCB / SOCB then test, clear and set that bit in the
* byte.
* PBIT - R2 = the address of page R0's bitmap byte, R4 = its mask (in the
* high byte).  Leaf.  Uses R2, R4, R5.
PBIT   MOV  R0,R2
       SRL  R2,3               byte = page / 8
       AI   R2,POOLBM
       MOV  R0,R5
       ANDI R5,7               bit = page mod 8
       LI   R4,>8000
       MOV  R5,R5
       JEQ  PB9
PB1    SRL  R4,1
       DEC  R5
       JNE  PB1
PB9    RT

* PALLOC - the lowest free page: R0 = its number, or 0 if the pool is
* empty.  The page is marked in use.  Uses R0-R5.  Leaf.
PALLOC MOV  R11,R3
       MOV  @FREECT,R0
       JEQ  PA8                none free
       MOV  @POOLLO,R0
PA1    C    R0,@POOLHI
       JHE  PA8
       BL   @PBIT
       MOVB *R2,R5
       COC  R4,R5              the page's bit set (free)?
       JEQ  PA2
       INC  R0
       JMP  PA1
PA2    SZCB R4,*R2             in use now
       DEC  @FREECT
       B    *R3
PA8    CLR  R0
       B    *R3

* PRELS - give page R0 back to the pool (it must have come from it).
* Releasing a page twice is harmless.  Uses R0-R5.  Leaf.
PRELS  MOV  R11,R3
       C    R0,@POOLLO         outside the pool's range: ignored
       JL   PR9
       C    R0,@POOLHI
       JHE  PR9
       BL   @PBIT
       MOVB *R2,R5
       COC  R4,R5              already free?
       JEQ  PR9
       SOCB R4,*R2
       INC  @FREECT
PR9    B    *R3
